<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maha Sura Singhanat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Chao Phraya Surasi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maha Sura Singhanat, born Bunma and known in the Thonburi period as Chao Phraya Surasi, was a Siamese general and the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>. With his brother he led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>'s campaigns against Lan Na, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, including the capture of Vientiane in 1779. After Rama I founded the Chakri dynasty in 1782 he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a>, or viceroy, and led the defence against the Burmese in the Nine Armies' War of 1785–1786.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-sura-singhanat.jpg" alt="Maha Sura Singhanat" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maha_Sura_Singhanat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bunma was born on 1 November 1744, the youngest child of Daoreung and of Thongdi, Royal Secretary of Northern Siam and Keeper of the Royal Seal, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosa-pan">Kosa Pan</a>, who led the Siamese mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 17th century. He entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> court as a mahadlek, a royal page. After the fall of Ayutthaya in 1767 he joined the forces of Phraya Tak, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a>, in the campaigns to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanthaburi">Chanthaburi</a> and drive the Burmese from Thonburi, and he brought his elder brother Thongduang, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>, into the king's inner circle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1770 he led a land force north against the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phra-fang">Chao Phra Fang</a> and was then made Chao Phraya Surasi Phitsanuwathirat, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, with charge of the northern frontier. Working with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila</a> of Lampang, he helped restore Chiang Mai under Siamese suzerainty, and earned the name Phraya Suea, the Tiger Lord. Accounts of the conquest of Vientiane place him in charge of the Lao captives brought to the capital.</p><p>After the coup of April 1782 that ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>'s reign, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a>, and he lived in the Front Palace complex north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> of the Konbaung dynasty invaded in 1785, he commanded at the Battle of Lat Ya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchanaburi">Kanchanaburi</a> against far larger numbers, harassing Burmese supply lines until they retreated. In 1786 he drove the Burmese from the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and won the Tha Din Daeng campaign. In 1802, marching to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila</a> at Chiang Mai, he fell ill with gallstones at Thoen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was also a poet. His works include a poem on the southern campaign of 1786, another written on the expedition to Tavoy in 1787, and a poem of 1795 on the enshrinement of the Phra Phuttha Sihing image in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a>. He died on 3 November 1803 and was cremated at Sanam Luang. A later tradition holds that he cursed anyone who held the Front Palace without being his descendant. He had 43 children, and the royal houses of Asuni, Sangkhatat, Patamasingh and Neerasingh descend from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maha Sura Singhanat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His generals, the brothers Chao Phraya Chakri and <b>Surasi</b>, expelled the Burmese from Lan Na and conquered the Kingdom of Vientiane in 1779, bringing back the Emerald Buddha.</blockquote><a href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.36,100.58&amp;year=1782" aria-label="Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maha Sura Singhanat"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5871 3785h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5871 3785h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.36,100.58&amp;year=1782">What was here in 1782? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
